lesson:
```
int clamp(int v, int lo, int hi)
{
    if (v < lo) return lo;
    if (v > hi) return hi;
    return v;
}
```

C reads top to bottom. To call a function that's written lower down, put a **prototype** (just the first line, with `;`) above:

```
int clamp(int v, int lo, int hi);   /* prototype */

int main(void) { printf("%d\n", clamp(120, 0, 100)); return 0; }

int clamp(int v, int lo, int hi) { ... }
```

Bigger programs put prototypes in a header (`sensor.h`) and the code in `sensor.c`. Arduino libraries are built exactly like that.

**Recursion**: a function can call itself, as long as it has a stopping case:

```
long factorial(int n)
{
    if (n <= 1) return 1;          /* stop */
    return n * factorial(n - 1);   /* smaller problem */
}
```

`long` holds bigger whole numbers than `int` (print it with `%ld`).

